<section aria-labelledby="phenomenon-first">
  <h2 id="phenomenon-first">The phenomenon is larger than zero resistance</h2>
  <p>
    The source begins with resistivity, critical fields, the Meissner effect, heat capacity, and acoustic attenuation. This is deliberate: superconductivity is not defined by a low calculated resistance alone. Persistent current and magnetic-flux expulsion identify an equilibrium electromagnetic phase, while thermodynamic anomalies and spectroscopic thresholds constrain its excitation structure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Type-I and type-II behavior further show that field penetration, coherence, vortices, and pinning introduce length and energy scales beyond a scalar transition temperature.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="three-theories">
  <h2 id="three-theories">London, Ginzburg–Landau, and BCS answer different questions</h2>
  <p>
    London theory gives a constitutive electrodynamics and a magnetic penetration depth. Ginzburg–Landau theory introduces a complex order parameter whose magnitude and phase vary in space; minimizing its free energy produces coherence and penetration lengths and the structure of vortices near its regime of validity. BCS theory constructs a microscopic paired state from fermions near the Fermi surface.
  </p>
  <p>
    Later theory connects the levels, but they are not the same statement. An order-parameter functional can remain useful beyond the simplest microscopic pairing model, and a microscopic calculation must still reproduce electromagnetic and thermodynamic behavior.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="paired-spectrum">
  <h2 id="paired-spectrum">Pairing reorganizes the excitation spectrum</h2>
  <p>
    BCS mixes empty and occupied pair configurations built from time-reversed states. The elementary quasiparticle energy becomes
  </p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="BCS quasiparticle dispersion">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mspace width="1em"/><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>μ</mi></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_&#123;&#92;mathbf k&#125;=&#92;sqrt&#123;&#92;xi_&#123;&#92;mathbf k&#125;^2+|&#92;Delta_&#123;&#92;mathbf k&#125;|^2&#125;,&#92;qquad &#92;xi_&#123;&#92;mathbf k&#125;=&#92;varepsilon_&#123;&#92;mathbf k&#125;-&#92;mu</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>
    ξ<sub>k</sub> measures normal-state energy from the chemical potential and Δ<sub>k</sub> is the pairing amplitude in the chosen channel. Where Δ is nonzero, the excitation spectrum avoids zero energy. The coherence factors describe how electron-like and hole-like amplitudes mix; they control both occupations and matrix elements.
  </p>
  <p class="paper-meta">Source locator: Cohen &amp; Louie, Chapter 14, §14.2.3, especially the BCS state and quasiparticle construction surrounding Figs. 14.24–14.33.</p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="gap-equation">
  <h2 id="gap-equation">The gap equation is a self-consistency condition</h2>
  <p>
    The pairing amplitude that determines the quasiparticle spectrum is itself generated by an anomalous pair expectation value: a coherent amplitude for adding or removing a time-reversed pair, rather than an ordinary one-particle occupation. The same paired state is determined by Δ and produces the pair amplitude used to update Δ. The BCS gap equation is therefore nonlinear: choose a pairing kernel, solve for Δ, rebuild the occupations and pair amplitude, and require consistency.
  </p>
  <p>
    A constant attractive interaction within a Debye window gives the familiar weak-coupling result and isotope-scale intuition. Real materials may have anisotropic, multiband, retarded, Coulomb-renormalized, or non-phononic kernels. A simplified cutoff is a model of the interaction, not a numerical convergence parameter.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="spectroscopic-tests">
  <h2 id="spectroscopic-tests">Thermodynamics and spectroscopy test the state</h2>
  <p>
    The paired spectrum changes entropy, free energy, heat capacity, quasiparticle DOS, tunneling conductance, and absorption. Tunneling can reveal a gap edge and coherence structure; electromagnetic or acoustic probes test different matrix elements. Consistent signatures across these observables are stronger evidence than fitting one broadened peak.
  </p>
  <p>
    A spectral gap can also arise from other orders or finite-size effects. Identifying superconductivity requires phase coherence and the relevant thermodynamic/electromagnetic evidence, not a gap alone.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="materials-boundary">
  <h2 id="materials-boundary">From a pairing kernel to a material claim</h2>
  <p>
    Electron–phonon matrix elements, phonon frequencies, and electronic phase space can be combined into Eliashberg-type spectral functions and approximate transition estimates. But large λ, one soft mode, or an interpolated Fermi-surface average does not prove superconductivity. Structural stability, Coulomb treatment, anisotropy, competing phases, convergence, and experimental evidence remain independent responsibilities.
  </p>
</section>
